{"name":"Roscoe Village","hex":"#998871","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"roscoe","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A handgun, particularly a revolver. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/roscoe"},{"term":"village","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rural habitation of size between a hamlet and a town.","A rural habitation that has a church, but no market."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/village"}],"entries":[{"title":"Roscoe Village (Coshocton, Ohio)","blocks":[{"text":"Roscoe Village is a restored Ohio and Erie Canal town located in Coshocton, Ohio, United States. Roscoe Village, was laid out in 1816. It was originally named Caldersburgh after its founder James Calder. After going bankrupt, the Coshocton merchant moved across the Muskingum River to some land he had managed to retain. Setting up a store and naming the place after himself, Calder reasoned that the rural farmers would much rather do business in Caldersburgh than pay the twenty-five cents for the ferry over to Coshocton. In 1830, two prominent citizens petitioned the state legislature to rename the village Roscoe in honor of William Roscoe, the famous English author and abolitionist of the time."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Roscoe_Village_from_the_south.jpg/330px-Roscoe_Village_from_the_south.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roscoe_Village_(Coshocton%2C_Ohio)","wikidata":"Q7367538"},{"title":"North Center, Chicago","blocks":[{"text":"North Center is one of the 77 community areas of Chicago in Illinois, United States, located in the city's North Side. North Center is bordered on the north by Montrose Avenue, on the south by Diversey Parkway, on the west by the Chicago River and on the east by Ravenswood Avenue; it includes the neighborhoods of Northcenter, Roscoe Village, St. Ben's, and Hamlin Park. The Brown Line of the Chicago 'L' has stops within the community area at Addison, Irving Park, and Montrose."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Lane_Tech_High_School_clock_tower.jpg/330px-Lane_Tech_High_School_clock_tower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Center%2C_Chicago","wikidata":"Q3344108"},{"title":"Village","blocks":[{"text":"A village is a human settlement or a residential community, larger than a hamlet but smaller than a town; some geographers describe villages as having between 500 and 2,500 inhabitants. Although villages are often located in rural areas, the term urban village is also applied to certain urban neighborhoods. Villages are normally permanent, with fixed dwellings, however, transient villages also exist. Further, the dwellings of a village are fairly close to one another, not scattered broadly over the landscape, as a dispersed settlement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Bourton-on-the-Water_2010_PD_09.JPG/330px-Bourton-on-the-Water_2010_PD_09.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Village","wikidata":"Q532"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Schoolhouse%2C_Roscoe_Village.jpg/500px-Schoolhouse%2C_Roscoe_Village.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Schoolhouse%2C_Roscoe_Village.jpg/1280px-Schoolhouse%2C_Roscoe_Village.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Schoolhouse%2C_Roscoe_Village.jpg/500px-Schoolhouse%2C_Roscoe_Village.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Interior of the schoolhouse","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASchoolhouse%2C_Roscoe_Village.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/US-IL-Chicago-NorthCenter.png/250px-US-IL-Chicago-NorthCenter.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/US-IL-Chicago-NorthCenter.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/US-IL-Chicago-NorthCenter.png/250px-US-IL-Chicago-NorthCenter.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUS-IL-Chicago-NorthCenter.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/New_%22Welcome_to_Roscoe_Village%22_Painted_Sign_on_Roscoe_at_the_Train_Tracks.jpg/500px-New_%22Welcome_to_Roscoe_Village%22_Painted_Sign_on_Roscoe_at_the_Train_Tracks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/New_%22Welcome_to_Roscoe_Village%22_Painted_Sign_on_Roscoe_at_the_Train_Tracks.jpg/1280px-New_%22Welcome_to_Roscoe_Village%22_Painted_Sign_on_Roscoe_at_the_Train_Tracks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/New_%22Welcome_to_Roscoe_Village%22_Painted_Sign_on_Roscoe_at_the_Train_Tracks.jpg/500px-New_%22Welcome_to_Roscoe_Village%22_Painted_Sign_on_Roscoe_at_the_Train_Tracks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Welcome to Roscoe Village\" sign on Roscoe at Metra overpass","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANew_%22Welcome_to_Roscoe_Village%22_Painted_Sign_on_Roscoe_at_the_Train_Tracks.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Veduta_di_Pacentro.jpg/500px-Veduta_di_Pacentro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipe